# 🔍 LogLens

**Plain-English log analysis with MITRE ATT&CK mapping, running serverless on AWS Lambda.**

LogLens helps students and early-career analysts make sense of security logs. Paste a Linux `auth.log` or a Windows Security event export, and LogLens flags brute-force attempts, suspicious logins and privilege abuse, then maps each finding to MITRE ATT&CK techniques so learners can connect raw log lines to attacker behavior.

**🌐 Live demo:** https://nt22iistdpdmzs3x3osvwzny7m0vmnyy.lambda-url.ap-southeast-2.on.aws/

Built for the **AWS Zero to Shipped Hackathon** (Workplace Efficiency category, Community track), using [Kiro](https://kiro.dev) as the coding agent connected to AWS.

## ✨ Features
- Auto-detects Linux `auth.log` vs Windows Security events
- Counts failed logins per source and flags brute force (5 or more failures)
- Raises **High** severity when a successful login follows repeated failures
- Additional Linux detections: invalid usernames, direct root login attempts, repeated failed `sudo`
- Windows detections: repeated failed logons (4625), successful logon after failures including RDP logon type 10 (4624), user account created (4720), and audit log cleared (1102)
- Each finding shows its **MITRE ATT&CK** technique with a link, first/last seen, and a short "why this matters" note
- **Copy as Markdown report** to share findings
- Built-in Linux and Windows sample logs to try instantly

## 🗺️ MITRE ATT&CK mapping
| Finding | Technique |
|---|---|
| Repeated failed logins | T1110.001 Brute Force: Password Guessing |
| Successful login after failures | T1078 Valid Accounts |
| Successful SSH login | T1021.004 Remote Services: SSH |
| Repeated failed sudo | T1548.003 Sudo and Sudo Caching |
| [Windows rules you implemented] | [technique IDs] |

## 🏗️ Architecture
```
Browser ──► Lambda Function URL ──► AWS Lambda (Python)
                                      ├─ GET /          → one-page UI
                                      └─ POST /analyze  → parse + detect + MITRE map
                                      └─► CloudWatch Logs
```
- **AWS Lambda** (Python, no external dependencies) serves both the page and the API
- **Lambda Function URL** provides the public endpoint
- **IAM execution role** with CloudWatch Logs permissions only
- No database, no servers

## 🔐 Security design
- Log content is treated as **untrusted input**: size cap, escaped output, nothing from the log is executed or fetched
- MITRE links are hardcoded, never built from log content
- The execution role is least-privilege (CloudWatch Logs only)
- The public invoke permission is limited to Function URL calls (`lambda:InvokedViaFunctionUrl`)
- Tested with a script-injection payload: no code runs

## 🚀 Run or deploy it yourself
1. Create a Lambda function using a current Python runtime [e.g. `python3.x`] and upload the contents of `lambda/`.
2. Set the handler to `[handler.lambda_handler, check your file]`.
3. Attach an execution role with CloudWatch Logs permissions only.
4. Create a Function URL with auth type `NONE`.
5. Add **both** permissions the public URL needs:
```bash
   aws lambda add-permission --function-name loglens \
     --statement-id UrlPolicyInvokeURL --action lambda:InvokeFunctionUrl \
     --principal "*" --function-url-auth-type NONE

   aws lambda add-permission --function-name loglens \
     --statement-id UrlPolicyInvokeFunction --action lambda:InvokeFunction \
     --principal "*" --invoked-via-function-url
```
6. Open the Function URL and click **Load Linux sample**.

⚠️ A public Function URL has no login. Use sample data only and don't paste real production logs.

## 🤖 How it was built
Built with **Kiro** and the **Agent Toolkit for AWS**, connected to my AWS account through a dedicated CLI profile. I chose the scope, reviewed every command before approving it, and tested each version in the browser. Features were added in stages (brute-force detection → MITRE mapping → more Linux detections → Windows events), with a rollback point saved before each upgrade.

**Challenges fixed along the way:** a Function URL `403 Forbidden` (missing `lambda:InvokeFunction` permission), terminal install loops, and a regex bug in sudo username extraction.

## 🛣️ What's next
- Amazon Bedrock explanations for unmatched lines (with prompt-injection protection and cost limits)
- Wazuh integration
- More log sources and saved scan history

## ⚠️ Disclaimer
LogLens is an educational tool built for learning. It uses simple rules and isn't a replacement for a SIEM or professional incident response.

